  "summary": "A cultural explosion centered in Harlem, NYC, celebrating Black identity through literature, music, visual arts, and performance.",
  "description": "Featured vibrant colors, modernist forms, African motifs, and everyday Black life, rejecting stereotypes while asserting cultural pride. Emerged as Great Migration brought African Americans to northern cities, creating a new Black consciousness.",
  "culturalContext": "The Harlem Renaissance (1918-1937) — the New Negro movement — built Black America's first full visual-aesthetic self-assertion: Aaron Douglas's silhouette murals fusing Art Deco with African mask geometry, Augusta Savage's sculpture, James Van Der Zee's parlor photography, cover design for The Crisis and Opportunity, and the Cabaret Calloway chitlin glamour. Patronage (Charlotte Mason, Alain Locke's The New Negro anthology) framed it as race advancement; its poster-deco-meets-Ancestry style remains the foundation of Black American graphic identity.",
